{
  "result": "On the morning the city took away the last public pay phone, someone called it.\n\nThe two workers looked at each other. One had already loosened the bolts. The other, whose name was Luis, picked up.\n\n\u201cIs it raining?\u201d a woman asked.\n\nLuis looked out through the scratched plastic. \u201cA little.\u201d\n\n\u201cCould you hold the phone outside?\u201d\n\nHe did. The cord was just long enough to reach beyond the hood. For a while he watched rain hit the receiver and wondered whether you could hear that from wherever she was.\n\n\u201cThank you,\u201d she said when he put it back to his ear. \u201cI used to wait for the bus there.\u201d\n\n\u201cWhich bus?\u201d\n\n\u201cThe fourteen.\u201d\n\n\u201cThey changed it to the six.\u201d\n\n\u201cOh,\u201d she said. Then, after a pause: \u201cDoes it still take forever?\u201d\n\nLuis looked down the avenue. His coworker had gone to sit in the truck, leaving the wrench on the curb.\n\n\u201cYeah,\u201d he said. \u201cIt still takes forever.\u201d\n\nAfter she hung up, he finished loosening the bolts. Under the phone, where the metal had met the wall, was a narrow strip of blue paint. He\u2019d worked on that block for eleven years and had only ever known the wall to be gray.\n\nHe called his coworker over to look.",
